Add a clock, a greeting and a quote of the day to the new tab page.

The three things a Tabliss-style new tab page offers that the speed dial and
the background chooser next to them do not, held to the standard those two
already set: nothing here is ever fetched. A live weather widget or a fetched
background needs a request this page has no business making by default - the
quote list below is compiled into the script instead, twenty-four lines of
text long enough out of copyright to need no licence, and the clock and
greeting read the browser's own clock. Same list as AeriumNtpWidgets.java on
the Android build, for the same reason it gives: a few quotes commonly found
to be misattributed (an "Aristotle" line on habit that is really Will
Durant's summary of him, an "Emerson" line absent from his actual writing, a
"Leonardo" line traced to a 1931 book about him) were left out rather than
repeated.

Rides in the same settings JSON blob as the background and layout choices -
cfg.clock, cfg.greeting, cfg.quote, cfg.name - for the reason the patch that
introduced that blob already gives: the page is the only reader and writer,
so a pref per checkbox would be four more registrations to say the same
thing an object key does. All three widgets default on; the name is empty by
default, and the greeting reads "Good morning" and similar without one.

Refreshed every 30 seconds while the page is open, the same interval and the
same reasoning as AeriumNtpWidgets' Handler on Android: readable, not exact
to the second, and cheap enough not to matter on a page people glance at and
leave.

Verified the same way aerium-newtab-page.patch's own settings addition was:
built against the tree that patch produces, the script re-extracted and run
through node --check, every new getElementById target cross-checked against
the ids added to the markup in this same patch, and the raw string rescanned
for a premature terminator.

